<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Todi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tuder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Todi, the ancient Tuder, is a hill town above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was an Umbrian town on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> and later a Roman colony, and the bronze Mars of Todi, an Etruscan-style statue of around 400 BCE, was found there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a free commune and the home of the poet Jacopone da Todi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/todi.jpg" alt="Todi" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Todi_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
